id: pm-9x9drr
title: "pm package manage --fix-managed-state holds the extension state lock across every remote update lookup, so a scan with several batches can outlast a concurrent package action's lock wait"
description: "Raised by CodeRabbit and Codex on PR 1752 (src/sdk/extension.ts, the prepareExploreManageState path). With --fix-managed-state, dispatchExtensionAction wraps the whole manage handler in withExtensionInstallLock. That handler adopts unmanaged packages and then calls refreshManagedExtensionUpdates, so the lock stays held while every npm view and git ls-remote runs, four at a time. The refresh never persists its result, so it does not need the lock. The lock wait is 30 seconds (120 attempts of 250 ms). As an interim mitigation, PR 1752 makes manage --fix-managed-state ignore PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS and keep the 10-second default, while the override (clamped to 25 seconds) applies everywhere else. At that default, more than eight managed npm or GitHub packages (three or more batches at concurrency four) can still keep the lock past a concurrent install, uninstall or adopt's 30-second wait, which then fails with a lock conflict. Proposed: keep only the adoption read and write under the lock, and run the refresh and runtime probe after it is released, while meeting the two design requirements recorded in this item's comments: a consistent freshness snapshot, and the host-error boundary."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 3
tags[4]: concurrency,extensions,locking,update-check
created_at: "2026-11-18T10:52:10.869Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-18T16:48:22.406Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 90
acceptance_criteria: "manage --fix-managed-state holds the extension state lock only for the adoption read and write; the remote update refresh and runtime probe run after the lock is released, with the same results and offline behaviour; a test proves a concurrent locked package action is not blocked by a slow lookup; manage without --fix-managed-state is unchanged"
parent: pm-ugqx
risk: medium
confidence: medium
severity: low
expected_result: A slow or long-budget update lookup never holds the extension state lock
actual_result: "With --fix-managed-state the lock spans the whole remote refresh at the default 10-second lookup budget (the override does not apply there, and permits are released as each lookup settles, so teardown adds no time under the lock), so three or more batches can exceed the 30-second lock wait"
component: extensions
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-5kfzjh,discovered_from,"2026-11-18T10:52:10.869Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
comments[5]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-18T11:09:40.162Z","harness:claude-code","Implemented in PR 1752. runExtensionExploreManageAction calls prepareExploreManageState(ctx, settings), which runs readAndAdoptExploreManageState (read managed state, list installed packages, adopt unmanaged ones) under withExtensionInstallLock only when manage adopts (--fix-managed-state). refreshManagedExtensionUpdates then runs after the lock is released. requiresExtensionStateLock no longer wraps manage --fix-managed-state at dispatch; doctor --fix-managed-state is unchanged. Nothing after adoption writes state: the second inventory listing, the runtime probe and triage only read. Test: tests/integration/extensions/manage-lock-scope.integration.spec.ts runs manage --fix-managed-state with a stalled npm on PATH, waits until the lookup has started, and acquires the scope lock with a single attempt, which succeeds. Negative control: moving the refresh back inside the lock makes that acquisition fail.\n"
  "2026-11-18T11:24:31.419Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation attempt on PR 1752 (67c7eef6b), reverted in the next commit. Codex review round 4 found two requirements that moving the lock alone does not meet, so this item stays open for its own change:\n1. Freshness snapshot consistency. Once the lock is released after adoption, a concurrent install can upgrade a package while its remote check runs. The second inventory scan then pairs the new manifest with update evidence computed from the old managed record, so a package just upgraded to latest can still be reported as having an update available. The fix must report one consistent snapshot, for example by re-reading managed state after the refresh and keeping update evidence only for entries whose installed identity (source, manifest_version, updated_at) is unchanged. A concurrently changed entry should report not checked rather than stale evidence.\n2. Host-error boundary. withExtensionInstallLock runs its body inside withHostEnvironmentBoundary, which turns EACCES and EMFILE from readdir into the stable, path-redacted refusal. Moving the inventory scan outside the lock also moved it outside that boundary, so it would reject with a raw Node error that carries the local path. The unlocked diagnostics need their own boundary.\nThe reverted design is still the right starting point: read state, list and adopt under the lock (readAndAdoptExploreManageState); refresh outside it; drop manage from requiresExtensionStateLock. Its test, which acquired the scope lock in one attempt while a stalled npm on PATH was running, and the negative control, which moved the refresh back under the lock and made the acquisition fail, both worked. Reuse them.\n"
  "2026-11-18T12:31:48.473Z","harness:claude-code","Mitigation shipped in PR 1752 while this item stays open: PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS is clamped to at most 25000 ms, below the 30-second lock wait (EXTENSION_INSTALL_LOCK_ATTEMPTS 120 times EXTENSION_INSTALL_LOCK_DELAY_MS 250). A single lookup batch under the manage --fix-managed-state lock therefore cannot outlast a concurrent install, uninstall or adoption. Several batches (more than four managed npm or GitHub packages, at the concurrency of 4) can still exceed it at the default budget. That is pre-existing and is what this item's structural fix, with the two requirements recorded in the previous comment, must remove.\n"
  "2026-11-18T13:00:20.956Z","harness:claude-code","Reproduction cases (from CodeRabbit on PR 1752):\n- Pre-existing, at the default budget: nine or more managed remote packages make three batches at concurrency four. If every lookup stalls, the refresh holds the lock for about 30 s or more, past a concurrent action's 30-second wait.\n- Introduced and then neutralised in PR 1752: with five managed remote packages (two batches), a raised PM_EXTENSION_UPDATE_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS of 25000 would have made the locked refresh take about 50 s, against about 20 s at the old fixed budget, turning a concurrent action that used to succeed into a lock conflict. PR 1752 prevents this by keeping the 10-second default for manage --fix-managed-state. The integration test asserts update_check_timeout_ms 10000 there with the override set to 2000.\nOnce this item moves the refresh out of the lock, the override can apply to --fix-managed-state as well.\n"
  "2026-11-18T16:48:22.405Z","harness:claude-code","Recorded from Codex round 21 on PR 1752: with the interim mitigation, the locked --fix-managed-state path releases a provider permit when its lookup settles, so it matches main's lock timing but can briefly have more than four providers alive while timed-out ones are being killed. Moving the refresh out of the lock (this item) lets permits be held through teardown without lengthening the lock hold.\n"
body: ""
